Subject: lib/glob.c: do not declare alloca() when it is a macro

Without GCC or <alloca.h>, glob.c declares "extern char *alloca ();".
VSI C defines alloca as a macro for its __ALLOCA built-in, so the
declaration does not parse (%CC-E-NOTEXPECTING).  A macro alloca needs
no declaration: skip it then.

--- a/lib/glob.c	2026-10-05 00:10:39.351085470 +0100
+++ b/lib/glob.c	2026-10-05 00:10:39.370290521 +0100
@@ -148,7 +148,7 @@
 #   ifndef _AIX
 #    ifdef WINDOWS32
 #     include <malloc.h>
-#    else
+#    elif !defined alloca
 extern char *alloca ();
 #    endif /* WINDOWS32 */
 #   endif /* Not _AIX.  */
